                <pb facs="1012" n="892" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg892"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/2494: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Warren</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-04-05T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 5, 1922—6
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>32. Department’s 31, April 7 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>], 6 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">7</hi>] p.m.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d702-f1"/> Advise
                    Stevens of contents of Peking’s No. 118, March 25, 1 p.m., in case you have
                    received it, otherwise Stevens will learn contents upon arrival at Harbin, and
                    also of the desire of the British Government to avoid further expense in
                    connection with the maintenance of the Technical Board. Ask him to discuss
                    situation fully with Wang and others <pb facs="1013" n="893" xml:id="pg893"/> as
                    he may think best, keeping in close touch with the Minister at Peking as well as
                    yourself and to write the Department at length his considered views respecting
                    future possibilities and American policy.</p>
                <p>Has Foreign Office spoken to you as forecast in Department’s instruction No. 41
                    of February 16?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
